# 12. INTERACTIVE FAMILY TREE BUILDER

This is the core feature of the entire platform.

Treat it as the most important engineering and user-experience component.

## Main Requirements

Build a beautiful, interactive, responsive genealogical tree.

The tree must support:

* Multiple generations.
* Parent-child relationships.
* Male and female family members.
* Spouses.
* Multiple valid marriage relationships.
* Descendants.
* Expanding branches.
* Collapsing branches.
* Searching.
* Selecting individuals.
* Navigating to ancestors.
* Navigating to descendants.
* Zooming.
* Panning.
* Fitting the tree to the viewport.
* Centering on a specific individual.
* Opening individual profiles.
* Adding new individuals.
* Proposing new branches.
* Displaying pending additions in the contribution workspace.

## Tree Visualization

Use elegantly styled individual nodes.

Each node should display:

* Full name or context-appropriate short name.
* Optional avatar.
* Optional small relationship indicator.
* Expand/collapse control where applicable.

The tree should use clear relationship connectors.

Do not represent the entire family as an arbitrary list of cards.

The interface must visually communicate actual genealogical relationships.

## Important Data Architecture

Family genealogy is not always a strict single-parent hierarchy.

Do not build the data model using only a single `parentId`.

Use a proper relationship model capable of representing:

* Father-child.
* Mother-child.
* Spouse relationships.
* Marriage-related metadata.
* Optional or unknown parents.
* Multiple generations.
* Complex family structures.

The visual tree may use hierarchical layouts, but the underlying data must preserve accurate relationships.

Do not automatically assume that every spouse is a biological parent of every child.

Support unknown or incomplete relationship information without inventing details.

Prevent circular parent-child relationships.

Prevent linking someone as their own ancestor.

## Large Tree Performance

Large family trees may contain thousands of individuals.

Avoid rendering all 10,000 nodes simultaneously.

Use:

* Branch-based loading.
* Progressive expansion.
* Viewport-based rendering where possible.
* Efficient graph layout.
* Caching.
* Memoization.
* Server-side pagination where appropriate.
* Optimized database queries.

The tree must remain usable on mobile devices.

